#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717560 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-3\\left(\\frac{-2-6\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}{3}\\right)-1$?" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the function to identify the whole coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=-3\\left(\\frac{-2-6\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}{3}\\right)-1 \\\\ &=\\frac{-3\\cdot(-6)}{3}\\cdot \\sin\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+\\frac{-3\\cdot(-2)}{3}-1 \\\\ &=6\\sin\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=6$, so the amplitude is $\\left|6\\right|=6$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717564 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=3\\left(\\frac{-6\\sin\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)-1}{5}\\right)+1$?" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the function to identify the whole coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=3\\left(\\frac{-6\\sin\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)-1}{5}\\right)+1 \\\\ &=\\frac{3\\cdot(-6)}{5}\\cdot \\sin\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+\\frac{3\\cdot(-1)}{5}+1 \\\\ &=-\\frac{18}{5}\\sin\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=-\\frac{18}{5}$, so the amplitude is $\\left|-\\frac{18}{5}\\right|=\\frac{18}{5}$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717576 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-3\\left(\\frac{3+5\\sin\\left(3x+1\\right)}{5}\\right)$?" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the function to identify the whole coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=-3\\left(\\frac{3+5\\sin\\left(3x+1\\right)}{5}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{-3\\cdot(5)}{5}\\cdot \\sin\\left(3x+1\\right)+\\frac{-3\\cdot(3)}{5} \\\\ &=-3\\sin\\left(3x+1\\right)-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=-3$, so the amplitude is $\\left|-3\\right|=3$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717579 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-2\\left(\\frac{3\\sin\\left(2x+1\\right)-1}{5}\\right)+1$?" :question/worked-solution "First, rewrite the function to identify the whole coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=-2\\left(\\frac{3\\sin\\left(2x+1\\right)-1}{5}\\right)+1 \\\\ &=\\frac{-2\\cdot(3)}{5}\\cdot \\sin\\left(2x+1\\right)+\\frac{-2\\cdot(-1)}{5}+1 \\\\ &=-\\frac{6}{5}\\sin\\left(2x+1\\right)+\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a function in the form $f(x)=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D$, the amplitude is $|A|$.\n\nHere, $A=-\\frac{6}{5}$, so the amplitude is $\\left|-\\frac{6}{5}\\right|=\\frac{6}{5}$. Horizontal transformations and additive constants do not change the amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717627 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2\\sin\\left(\\frac{3}{2}x+60^{\\circ}\\right)+1.\n$$\n\nAngles are measured in degrees." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficient of $x$ inside the sine:\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe period is a positive length. One full turn is $360^{\\circ}$ in degrees.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{|B|}=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{\\frac{3}{2}}=240^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe outside multiplier and the constant shifts do not change the period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717641 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sin\\left(\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)+3.\n$$\n\nAngles are measured in radians." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficient of $x$ inside the sine:\n\n$$\nB=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe period is a positive length. One full turn is $2\\pi$ in radians.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi}{|B|}=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\frac{2}{3}}=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe outside multiplier and the constant shifts do not change the period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717643 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3\\cos\\left(9x-30^{\\circ}\\right).\n$$\n\nAngles are measured in degrees." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficient of $x$ inside the cosine:\n\n$$\nB=9.\n$$\n\nThe period is a positive length. One full turn is $360^{\\circ}$ in degrees.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{|B|}=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{9}=40^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe outside multiplier and the constant shifts do not change the period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717646 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2\\cos\\left(6x-60^{\\circ}\\right)-4.\n$$\n\nAngles are measured in degrees." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficient of $x$ inside the cosine:\n\n$$\nB=6.\n$$\n\nThe period is a positive length. One full turn is $360^{\\circ}$ in degrees.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{|B|}=\\frac{360^{\\circ}}{6}=60^{\\circ}.\n$$\n\nThe outside multiplier and the constant shifts do not change the period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717647 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\cos\\left(8x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-4.\n$$\n\nAngles are measured in radians." :question/worked-solution "Read the coefficient of $x$ inside the cosine:\n\n$$\nB=8.\n$$\n\nThe period is a positive length. One full turn is $2\\pi$ in radians.\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi}{|B|}=\\frac{2\\pi}{8}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe outside multiplier and the constant shifts do not change the period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717658 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-12$ and a maximum value of $-2$." :question/worked-solution "The midline is the horizontal line halfway between the minimum and maximum values. Average their heights:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{y_{\\text{min}}+y_{\\text{max}}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-12+(-2)}{2} \\\\\n&= -7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=-7$. The half-range $5$ is the amplitude, rather than the vertical shift."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717665 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-4$ and a maximum value of $4$." :question/worked-solution "The midline is the horizontal line halfway between the minimum and maximum values. Average their heights:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{y_{\\text{min}}+y_{\\text{max}}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4+4}{2} \\\\\n&= 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=0$. The half-range $4$ is the amplitude, rather than the vertical shift."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717669 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-5$ and a maximum value of $1$." :question/worked-solution "The midline is the horizontal line halfway between the minimum and maximum values. Average their heights:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{y_{\\text{min}}+y_{\\text{max}}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5+1}{2} \\\\\n&= -2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=-2$. The half-range $3$ is the amplitude, rather than the vertical shift."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717674 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $0$ and a maximum value of $8$." :question/worked-solution "The midline is the horizontal line halfway between the minimum and maximum values. Average their heights:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{y_{\\text{min}}+y_{\\text{max}}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0+8}{2} \\\\\n&= 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=4$. The half-range $4$ is the amplitude. Here the amplitude and midline height happen to have the same numerical value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717680 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-1$ and a maximum value of $7$." :question/worked-solution "The midline is the horizontal line halfway between the minimum and maximum values. Average their heights:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{y_{\\text{min}}+y_{\\text{max}}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1+7}{2} \\\\\n&= 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=3$. The half-range $4$ is the amplitude, rather than the vertical shift."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717698 :question/problem "![Vector field following elliptical streamlines and a clockwise path](images/af/af4cc83cbf80aad55631338df3d6fd7e348ef9d3eefd6b7d107712af2010e20d.png)\n\nThe gray curves are streamlines of $\\mathbf F$: the field follows these ellipses, including the blue ellipse $S$. Let $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ denote the reversed traversal. In both cases, flux is measured with the outward-pointing normal.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Flux means the net flow through the boundary, measured using the outward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n$:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward flow contributes positively, inward flow negatively, and tangential flow contributes zero.\n\nAlong $S$, each field vector is tangent to the ellipse and perpendicular to its outward normal. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n=0$ at every boundary point: the flow moves along the boundary without crossing it.\n\n![Tangential field vectors and outward normals on the ellipse](images/76/7667f7e5229779efaff5694f34d926be0569e293ab47a3dc6695272fe73032b0.png)\n\nIt follows that $\\Phi_C=0$. Reversing traversal leaves the outward normals unchanged, so $\\Phi_{-C}=0$ as well. The clockwise direction of $C$ does not make outward flux negative.\n\n- The flux across $C$ is zero: **true**.\n- The flux across $-C$ is zero: **true**.\n- The flux across $C$ is negative: **false**.\n\nSelect both zero-flux statements."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717707 :question/problem "![Inward vector field and a counterclockwise circular path](images/43/4374424f671e1e85688fc92368d0f1cdb4b3310b10bb6ee781d45936b5b0fca4.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ denote the reversed traversal. In both cases, flux is measured with the outward-pointing normal.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Flux means the net flow through the boundary, measured using the outward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n$:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward flow contributes positively, inward flow negatively, and tangential flow contributes zero.\n\nThe field points into the circle at every boundary point. Its component along the outward normal is negative, so there is net inflow and $\\Phi_C<0$.\n\n![Field vectors oppose the outward normals](images/d6/d6f1a4ddeffb19a26c2b1da8230f348e498df88451f2eb82302ab33433b68a8c.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore $\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0$.\n\n- The flux across $-C$ is positive: **false**.\n- The flux across $C$ is zero: **false**.\n- The flux across $C$ is negative: **true**.\n\nSelect only the statement that the flux across $C$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717713 :question/problem "![Combined uniform and circulating vector field around a circle](images/e9/e9073825b7a850bbd7744f7047ed779c18a9cf3c9f1136991fffd5b4ff465c8d.png)\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F$ is formed by adding a uniform rightward flow to a counterclockwise flow tangent to every circle centered at $O$. The blue circle $S$ is centered at $O$. The arrows show the combined field.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ denote the reversed traversal. In both cases, flux is measured with the outward-pointing normal.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Flux means the net flow through the boundary, measured using the outward-pointing unit normal $\\mathbf n$:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward flow contributes positively, inward flow negatively, and tangential flow contributes zero.\n\nConsider the two component flows separately. The outward component of their sum is the sum of their outward components.\n\n![Uniform flow crosses opposite arcs equally; circulating flow is tangent](images/39/397d4353134f6638059fadddcbad3e0bdbc210a07cdeea10d93bdf36c8c2afa0.png)\n\nThe circulating component is tangent to $S$, so it contributes zero flux at every point.\n\nThe uniform rightward component enters through the left semicircle and exits through the right semicircle. Each small arc on the left has a reflected arc of equal length on the right. The horizontal components of their outward normals are equal in magnitude and opposite in sign. Since the field is uniform, the inward and outward contributions cancel in pairs.\n\nThus the total flux is zero. This does **not** mean the combined field is tangent everywhere: for example, it crosses outward at the rightmost point and inward at the leftmost point.\n\nReversing traversal leaves the outward normals unchanged, so\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\Phi_{-C}=0.\n$$\n\n- The flux across $C$ is positive: **false**.\n- The flux across $-C$ is zero: **true**.\n- The flux across $C$ is negative: **false**.\n\nSelect only the zero-flux statement."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717789 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=xy\\,\\mathbf i+(x-y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown above. Its right, top, left, and bottom sides are $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write the outward flux in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_{C_1}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_4}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n$? Leave each expression in terms of $x$ and $y$ before substituting side equations. You do not need to evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "Find the outward unit normal separately on each side. The right and left sides have normals $\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf i;$ the top and bottom sides have normals $\\mathbf j$ and $-\\mathbf j.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf i=xy,\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf j=x-y,\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=-xy,\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=-(x-y)=y-x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe full flux expression is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_{C_1}xy\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}(x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}(-xy)\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_4}(y-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the correct ordered quadruple is $(xy,x-y,-xy,y-x).$ Opposite-side terms are integrated over different sides, so their displayed signs do not justify canceling the integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717792 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=4xy\\,\\mathbf i-2x^2\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2=4.$ If the outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\boxed{\\phantom{x^2y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression? You do not need to evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\mathbf j.$*" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nso the missing expression is the dot product with the supplied unit normal.\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nMultiply corresponding components and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\n&=(4xy)\\frac{x}{2}+(-2x^2)\\frac{y}{2}\\\\\n&=2x^2y-x^2y\\\\\n&=x^2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux is $\\oint_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}s,$ and the missing expression is $x^2y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717793 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^2+y)\\,\\mathbf i+xy\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown above. If the outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the left side $C_3$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_3}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression? Leave it in terms of $x$ and $y$ without substituting the equation of the side or evaluating the integral." :question/worked-solution "The integrand is $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n,$ where $\\mathbf n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal. On the left side $C_3,$ outward is to the left, so $\\mathbf n=-\\mathbf i.$\n\n![](images/c6/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\n&=\\bigl((x^2+y)\\,\\mathbf i+xy\\,\\mathbf j\\bigr)\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)\\\\\n&=-(x^2+y)\\\\\n&=-x^2-y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $\\mathbf j$ component is tangent to this side and contributes zero. Therefore, the required expression is $-x^2-y,$ and the flux through $C_3$ is $\\int_{C_3}(-x^2-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717814 :question/problem "Find the range of $f(x)=-4\\sin\\left(-4x\\right)+1$ for all real $x$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1&\\le \\sin\\left(-4x\\right)\\le 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by $-4$, reversing both inequalities. Then add $1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4&\\ge -4\\sin\\left(-4x\\right)\\ge -4 \\\\5&\\ge -4\\sin\\left(-4x\\right)+1\\ge -3 \\\\-3&\\le f(x)\\le 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\\in[-3,5]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717819 :question/problem "Find the range of $f(x)=-3\\sin\\left(-3x+60^{\\circ}\\right)-4$ for all real $x$ (angles in degrees)." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1&\\le \\sin\\left(-3x+60^{\\circ}\\right)\\le 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by $-3$, reversing both inequalities. Then subtract $4$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3&\\ge -3\\sin\\left(-3x+60^{\\circ}\\right)\\ge -3 \\\\-1&\\ge -3\\sin\\left(-3x+60^{\\circ}\\right)-4\\ge -7 \\\\-7&\\le f(x)\\le -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\\in[-7,-1]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717820 :question/problem "Find the range of $f(x)=-3\\sin\\left(-2x\\right)+3$ for all real $x$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1&\\le \\sin\\left(-2x\\right)\\le 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by $-3$, reversing both inequalities. Then add $3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3&\\ge -3\\sin\\left(-2x\\right)\\ge -3 \\\\6&\\ge -3\\sin\\left(-2x\\right)+3\\ge 0 \\\\0&\\le f(x)\\le 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\\in[0,6]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717836 :question/problem "Find the range of $f(x)=-2\\cos\\left(-4x+2\\right)+2$ for all real $x$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1&\\le \\cos\\left(-4x+2\\right)\\le 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by $-2$, reversing both inequalities. Then add $2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2&\\ge -2\\cos\\left(-4x+2\\right)\\ge -2 \\\\4&\\ge -2\\cos\\left(-4x+2\\right)+2\\ge 0 \\\\0&\\le f(x)\\le 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\\in[0,4]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717841 :question/problem "Find the range of $f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-4x+\\pi\\right)-1$ for all real $x$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero horizontal scale and any horizontal shift preserve the full sine or cosine range over all real inputs. Start with\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1&\\le \\cos\\left(-4x+\\pi\\right)\\le 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by $-4$, reversing both inequalities. Then subtract $1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4&\\ge -4\\cos\\left(-4x+\\pi\\right)\\ge -4 \\\\3&\\ge -4\\cos\\left(-4x+\\pi\\right)-1\\ge -5 \\\\-5&\\le f(x)\\le 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth endpoint values and every value between them are attained. Therefore, the range is $f(x)\\in[-5,3]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717955 :question/problem "![Vector field and square boundary](images/c1/c1ddc181913de94b2c95b7b372a5db4124b1d47c6b592aa3195e2ca25208bafd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=-x\\,\\mathbf i-y\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown above. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^4\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n_k$ is the outward-pointing unit normal on side $C_k$.\n\n![Square sides and outward unit normals](images/76/7654d3ef80bdb0e864089738817c440605c84dcd5a9b47bc063b34f8d3c5f3da.png)\n\nFirst consider the right side $C_1$, where $x=1$ and $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$. A parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\qquad -1\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j$, we have $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=1$ and $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. Along this side,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\n=(-\\mathbf i-t\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Phi_1=\\int_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\mathrm dt=-2.\n$$\n\nRotating the square and this inward radial field by a quarter-turn preserves the normal component and side length, so each side contributes $-2$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4\\Phi_1=4(-2)=-8.\n$$\n\nThe negative sign indicates net inflow. Opposite sides add here: both the field and the outward normal reverse, leaving their dot product unchanged."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717962 :question/problem "![Vector field and square boundary](images/9b/9bd4a824dff41012a480d6bb56d0ab264138ca660ab3ad0e2e086cf09e5a4e9e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=-\\mathbf i$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown above. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$." :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^4\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n_k$ is the outward-pointing unit normal on side $C_k$.\n\n![Square sides and outward unit normals](images/76/7654d3ef80bdb0e864089738817c440605c84dcd5a9b47bc063b34f8d3c5f3da.png)\n\nThe field is parallel to the top and bottom sides, so its normal component is zero there:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_2=\\Phi_4=0.\n$$\n\nOn the right side, $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$, so $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1=-1$. On the left side, $\\mathbf n_3=-\\mathbf i$, so $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3=1$. Each side has length $2$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_1=(-1)(2)=-2,\\qquad \\Phi_3=(1)(2)=2.\n$$\n\nThe inflow on the right cancels the equal outflow on the left. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\Phi=-2+0+2+0=0.\n$$\n\nZero total flux does not mean the field has zero flow through every side."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717963 :question/problem "![Vector field and square boundary](images/54/54d522ffa63b12cbf84269753bfe641735997ecc86874c77adfa26e3ef0898be.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(x-2)\\,\\mathbf i+(y+1)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown above. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^4\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n_k$ is the outward-pointing unit normal on side $C_k$.\n\n![Square sides and outward unit normals](images/76/7654d3ef80bdb0e864089738817c440605c84dcd5a9b47bc063b34f8d3c5f3da.png)\n\nThe full field does not give equal flux through all four sides. Instead, separate it into a radial field and a uniform field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U,\\qquad\n\\mathbf R=x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j,\\qquad\n\\mathbf U=-2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, their flux contributions add.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite sides have opposite normals and equal lengths. Its right, top, left and bottom contributions are $-4$, $2$, $4$ and $-2$, respectively. These cancel, so $\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=0$.\n\nFor the radial field $\\mathbf R$, parametrize the right side by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-1\\le t\\le1$. Here $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=1$, giving\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_{-1}^{1}(\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm dt\n=\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\mathrm dt=2.\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry gives the same radial contribution on every side. Hence\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(2)=8,\\qquad \\Phi=\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}+\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=8+0=8.\n$$\n\nAs a check, the full field has the following contributions:\n\n| Side | $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n$ | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| Top | $2$ | $4$ |\n| Left | $3$ | $6$ |\n| Bottom | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nThese sum to $-2+4+6+0=8$. Multiplying the full field's right-side flux by four would be invalid; the fourfold symmetry applies to $\\mathbf R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717979 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=xe^{xy},$ $x=t^2,$ and $y=\\sin t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ in terms of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![Two chain-rule paths from f through x and y to t](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe two paths in the tree contribute products, which we add:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative with respect to $x$ needs the product rule as well as the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}=(1+xy)e^{xy},\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=x^2e^{xy}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=(1+t^2\\sin t)e^{t^2\\sin t},\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=t^4e^{t^2\\sin t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=2t,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\cos t.\n$$\n\nBoth coordinate derivatives must multiply their corresponding partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=(1+t^2\\sin t)e^{t^2\\sin t}(2t)+t^4e^{t^2\\sin t}\\cos t\\\\\n&=e^{t^2\\sin t}(2t+2t^3\\sin t+t^4\\cos t).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717983 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x}{1+y},$ $x=\\sin(t^2),$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ in terms of $t.$\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![Two chain-rule paths from f through x and y to t](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe two paths in the tree contribute products, which we add:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to one intermediate variable at a time:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=\\frac{1}{1+y},\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-\\frac{x}{(1+y)^2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\sin(t^2)$ and $y=e^t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=\\frac{1}{1+e^t},\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-\\frac{\\sin(t^2)}{(1+e^t)^2}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $x$ requires the inner derivative of $t^2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=2t\\cos(t^2),\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=e^t.\n$$\n\nMultiply along each path, then add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\frac{2t\\cos(t^2)}{1+e^t}-\\frac{e^t\\sin(t^2)}{(1+e^t)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2t(1+e^t)\\cos(t^2)-e^t\\sin(t^2)}{(1+e^t)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two displayed forms are equivalent. Since $1+e^t>0,$ the derivative is defined for every real $t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717985 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\cos y,$ $x=3t,$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ in terms of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![Two chain-rule paths from f through x and y to t](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe two paths in the tree contribute products, which we add:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nFirst find the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=\\cos y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-x\\sin y.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=3t$ and $y=t$ into these expressions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=\\cos t,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-3t\\sin t.\n$$\n\nThe coordinate derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=3,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=1.\n$$\n\nMultiply along each path, then add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=(\\cos t)(3)+(-3t\\sin t)(1)\\\\\n&=3(\\cos t-t\\sin t).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718063 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^2+xy$ and $y=\\cos x$, compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is the independent variable and $y=y(x)$, the two contributions are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nHolding the other variable fixed in each partial derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2x+y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=\\cos x$ into these expressions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2x+\\cos x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the dependency:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin x.\n$$\n\nAdd the two chain-rule contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=(2x+\\cos x)+x(-\\sin x)\\\\&=2x+\\cos x-x\\sin x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first term accounts for the explicit dependence on $x$; the second accounts for the change through $y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718066 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{y}{x+1}$ and $y=x\\sin x$, where $x\\ne-1$. Compute the total derivative in terms of $x$.\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=$ {{derivative}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is the independent variable and $y=y(x)$, the two contributions are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nHolding $y$ fixed when differentiating with respect to $x$, and $x$ fixed when differentiating with respect to $y$, gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=-\\frac{y}{(x+1)^2},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nAlong $y=x\\sin x$, these become\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=-\\frac{x\\sin x}{(x+1)^2},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUse the product rule for the dependency:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sin x+x\\cos x.\n$$\n\nCombine the two contributions over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=-\\frac{x\\sin x}{(x+1)^2}+\\frac{\\sin x+x\\cos x}{x+1}\\\\&=\\frac{-x\\sin x+(x+1)(\\sin x+x\\cos x)}{(x+1)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{\\sin x+x(x+1)\\cos x}{(x+1)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is valid for $x\\ne-1$, as required by the original denominator."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718072 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^2+xy$ and $y=x\\sin x$, compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is the independent variable and $y=y(x)$, the two contributions are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nHolding the other variable fixed gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2x+y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=x\\sin x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2x+x\\sin x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x.\n$$\n\nThe dependency is a product, so both factors contribute to its derivative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=1\\cdot\\sin x+x\\cos x.\n$$\n\nNow apply the special-case chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=(2x+x\\sin x)+x(\\sin x+x\\cos x)\\\\&=2x+x\\sin x+x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x\\\\&=2x+2x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first term accounts for the explicit dependence on $x$; the second accounts for the change through $y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718089 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy^2-2y$, where $x=st^2$ and $y=s^2-t$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$." :question/worked-solution "![Chain-rule paths to s](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nHold $t$ fixed. Multiply the derivatives along each highlighted path to $s$, then add the two products.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives, treating $x$ and $y$ as independent.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=y^2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=2xy-2.\n$$\n\nWrite these derivatives in terms of $s$ and $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=(s^2-t)^2,\\\\\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2st^2(s^2-t)-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $s$, holding $t$ fixed.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}=t^2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}=2s.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the chain rule and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}&=(s^2-t)^2t^2+\\bigl[2st^2(s^2-t)-2\\bigr](2s)\\\\&=(s^4t^2-2s^2t^3+t^4)+(4s^4t^2-4s^2t^3-4s)\\\\&=5s^4t^2-6s^2t^3+t^4-4s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718091 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x^2-3y$, where $x=st$ and $y=s^2t$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$." :question/worked-solution "![Chain-rule paths to s](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nHold $t$ fixed. Multiply the derivatives along each highlighted path to $s$, then add the two products.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives, treating $x$ and $y$ as independent.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-3.\n$$\n\nWrite these derivatives in terms of $s$ and $t$.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2st,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-3.\n$$\n\nNext differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $s$, holding $t$ fixed.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}=t,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}=2st.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the chain rule and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}&=(2st)(t)+(-3)(2st)\\\\&=2st^2-6st\\\\&=2st(t-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718095 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2x-y$, where $x=s+3t$ and $y=2s-t$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/worked-solution "![Chain-rule paths to t](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. Multiply the derivatives along each highlighted path to $t$, then add the two products.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives, treating $x$ and $y$ as independent.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-1.\n$$\n\nNext differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $t$, holding $s$ fixed.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}=3,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the chain rule and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=(2)(3)+(-1)(-1)\\\\&=6+1\\\\&=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718108 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/01/015f414e673c7484833a3338ed6520ddc5f7d7e212643c5d0e3dff03978c0186.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. $\\mathbf F$ expands horizontally and compresses vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The expansion and compression rates are equal.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf F$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. Their normal contributions are equal and opposite, so they cancel. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ is zero even though the field crosses the boundary.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/f9/f9643ae31e16b22cf0b82d817a3e473bcf404b66287c9cad6fda352da1314bcb.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C=0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive. **false**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718118 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/ee/ee900d037687ed35815a9cc5753cd829c014f307c970eeea91342c7aa2d743c9.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. The blue boundary and gray reference curves are concentric circles. Boundary crossing directions and tangencies shown by the arrows hold between the sampled points as well.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nThe field has motion along the boundary, but its arrows also cross consistently inward. Tangential motion contributes no flux. The normal component is negative all around the boundary, so the flux of $\\mathbf F$ is negative.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/01/01f72b9b0266cb8c3cd36ada6aac37050fee3239735c485dd5d3d7ba5347ef97.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **true**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718126 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/c0/c09f161967ce1a816fa8ac6003358594a9d409a56d6480a730003cd2fcd4f38c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. $\\mathbf F$ expands horizontally and compresses vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The expansion rate is three times the other rate.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf F$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. The expanding contribution is three times as large as the opposing contribution. There is net outflow, so the flux of $\\mathbf F$ is positive.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/37/37d4431e42b8defeb89e4d0d241aa444459f79902c6b7eaf454397703e09d76b.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C>0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero. **true**.\n"}]}
